\section{Where a value sits in the vertical}{

FVCOM uses \strong{sigma coordinates}: each of the 45 layers is a fixed \emph{fraction}
of the local water column rather than a fixed depth, so layer 1 is the surface
everywhere and layer 45 the sea floor everywhere. That is why \code{SST} and \code{BOTT}
are two entries reading one variable at two layers, and why bottom salinity
costs nothing here — \code{BOTS} is simply \code{salinity} at the bottom layer, where in
GLORYS it has to be derived. See \code{\link[=copernicus_variables]{copernicus_variables()}}.

The flip side is that a sigma layer is not a depth. Layer 45 sits at 98.9\% of
the local depth, which is a metre off the bottom on the shelf and fifty
metres off it in the Northeast Channel.
}

\section{Nodes and elements}{

An FVCOM mesh carries scalars on triangle \strong{nodes} and velocities on triangle
\strong{elements} (the centroids). These are two different sets of points — 48,451
and 90,415 on GOM3 — so a variable of each kind cannot land in one table
without interpolating one onto the other.

\code{\link[=accessFVCOM]{accessFVCOM()}} refuses to fetch the two together rather than interpolating on
your behalf, in the same spirit as \code{\link[=accessEnvDat]{accessEnvDat()}} refusing to mix two
Copernicus grids. Fetch each and chain \code{\link[=matchData]{matchData()}}, which matches to the
nearest point whichever mesh it belongs to.
}

